---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { storyBySlug, storyTitle } from '../../data/stories';
import doorScene1 from '../../assets/security-door-scene1.webp';
import doorScene2 from '../../assets/security-door-scene2.webp';
import doorScene4 from '../../assets/security-door-scene4.webp';
import doorScene6 from '../../assets/security-door-scene6.webp';
import doorScene9 from '../../assets/security-door-scene9.webp';
import doorScene11 from '../../assets/security-door-scene11.webp';

// The Security story's permanent Explore route. Thirteen beats.
//
// The opening construction is the one looks-real-is-it established and
// who-chose-what-you-saw reuses: one grid, the copy on the left and the
// picture on the right, centred against each other, stacking copy-first
// below 980px. Same shell, same spacing scale, same serif sizes, same
// turn treatment — nothing global is touched here.
//
// SCENE 1. The card has already said the sentence; the page says it again
// as its own opening, because the second half is the part that has to
// land slowly:
//
//     Security didn't keep changing.
//     What you trusted did.
//     And you probably didn't notice when it happened.
//     Start with your front door →
//
// Nothing is explained and nothing is named. The picture is a front door
// someone is knocking at. The circles around it are cropped and quieted
// on purpose: the visitor should be asking why this ordinary door
// matters, not reading a diagram.
//
// SCENE 2. Everything scene 1 put around the door is taken away, and what
// is left is the door:
//
//     You leave home.
//     You close the door.
//     You lock it.
//     Then you walk away.
//     Why? →
//
// Four things the reader has done a thousand times, in the order they do
// them, and then the question. Nothing is named and nothing is explained,
// because the point of this beat is the answer the reader gives
// themselves before the story gives them one.
//
// The picture is the other supplied asset, whole: an ordinary front door
// on its own. Going back to a plain door after the first scene's
// ecosystem is the beat — the complexity was real, and it is now
// entirely out of sight.
//
// SCENE 3. The same door, closer:
//
//     Because you trust the lock.
//     You don't stay behind to guard the door.
//     You expect the lock to do its job after you've walked away.
//     Then someone else needs to get in →
//
// The one idea the story is willing to name yet is trust, and this is
// where it is named. Still nothing about what a lock is, what it can
// fail at, or who else is involved.
//
// The picture does not change: it is the same element and the same file
// as scene 2, moved in on by a transform rather than swapped for a
// second crop. That is the whole point of the beat — the reader has not
// been shown a new picture, they have stepped closer to the one they
// were already looking at. See .door__frame in the styles.
//
// SCENE 4. Someone else is at the door:
//
//     A pipe starts leaking.
//     You call a plumber.
//     You won't be home when he arrives.
//     So you leave him a key.
//     What changed? →
//
// Four ordinary decisions, none of them careless, and the story does not
// say what they add up to. The reader is the one who notices that
// somebody who is not them can now open the door — which is why the turn
// asks rather than tells.
//
// The picture is the same porch at the same size, with a person at it:
// the file is the Frontdoor asset's exact dimensions, so it lands in the
// window the door already occupied and the beat reads as someone
// arriving at a place the reader knows, not as a new picture. He is
// deliberately unremarkable — work clothes, a toolbag, reaching for the
// handle.
//
// SCENE 5. The first callback to the opening, in the opening's own shape:
//
//     The lock didn't change.
//     Who you trusted did.
//     The same key still opens the same door.
//     But now someone else can use it.
//     Then he arrives →
//
// Deliberately not the opening sentence repeated — the same couplet
// construction with different words, so the rhyme is felt before it is
// recognised. Still no mechanism, still nothing named beyond trust.
//
// The picture stays the plumber and steps forward slightly. The move is
// small on purpose: anything strong enough to actually re-centre on the
// man would have to cut the door or a planter in half, and this beat
// needs the place unchanged, because that is what it says. The sentence
// moves the attention, not the camera.
//
// SCENE 6. Two people are on the step:
//
//     He arrives.
//     But he isn't alone.
//     He brought someone to help.
//     You've never met him.
//     And yet he's about to enter your home. →
//
// Nothing is explained and nothing is named. The second man is in the
// same uniform, carrying the same kind of bag, doing nothing unusual —
// the discomfort has to come from the arithmetic, not from how he looks.
//
// The picture is the third supplied asset, the same porch at the same
// 1122x1402 frame with a second person on it, shown whole and unpushed:
// scene 5 had stepped in slightly, and coming back out is what makes room
// for somebody who was not expected.
//
// NOTE ON THAT FILE. It arrived as a PNG with no alpha — a white matte
// where the other two are cut out. Left as supplied it would render as a
// white rectangle on the page's cream, so the matte was lifted before
// conversion: a flood fill inward from the border over connected
// near-white pixels, with a one-pixel feather. Border-connected only, so
// the lit glass and the lamp are untouched, and no pixel of the subject
// was altered. Composition, colour and framing are exactly as delivered.
//
// SCENE 7. The idea the story has been walking toward, said plainly and
// still not named:
//
//     You didn't choose him.
//     You didn't check him.
//     You don't even know him.
//     But the plumber does.
//     So now you're trusting someone
//     because someone you trust trusts him.
//     And that's only one step away →
//
// The last sentence is the beat, so it gets the story's serif at full
// ink a step down from the headline (.door__lands) and the turn waits
// an extra moment before it appears — the line should be read before
// there is a way onward. No terminology: not vendor, not third party,
// not fourth party, not supply chain. The reader gets the idea first;
// the word for it can come later.
//
// The picture moves onto the two of them — same file, a transform on
// the element already on screen. The door is still behind them but its
// pediment, the lamp and the far planter fall outside the frame, so the
// pair is what the scene is about and the door is what they are standing
// at. Nothing is drawn between them: no arrow, no line, no diagram. The
// sentence is the only thing connecting them.
//
// SCENE 8. The idea stated as plainly as it can be, and still not named:
//
//     Trust can travel.
//     You chose the plumber.
//     He chose someone else.
//     And his choice entered your home too.
//     Now put the key away →
//
// Three sentences and an arithmetic the reader has already done. Still no
// terminology — not vendor, not third or fourth party, not supply chain,
// not dependency, not risk.
//
// The picture eases back out to the window scene 5 used: the whole
// ordinary porch, both men well inside it, nothing singled out and
// nothing drawn between them. Returning to the framing of the beat that
// asked who you trusted is the quiet rhyme — the same view, with two
// people in it now.
//
// SCENE 9. The upgrade, and it is allowed to feel like one:
//
//     You replace the lock.
//     No key this time.
//     Your phone opens the door.
//     It feels simpler.
//     But something else just changed. →
//
// Nothing here says software, network, cloud, vendor or risk. On this
// beat the change genuinely is a convenience — the reader should like it.
// Only the turn hints that there is more to it, and it does not say what.
//
// The picture is the fourth supplied asset: the same door with a keypad
// lock above the handle and a phone held up to it. One change and no
// other: no cloud, no signal, no badge, no diagram, nothing drawn on it.
//
// It is 1181x1332 where the other three door assets are 1122x1402, so it
// sits beside the shared window rather than inside it and keeps its own
// proportions — a frame built for 0.8 would have letterboxed or cropped
// it. Nothing else about the composition changes.
//
// SCENE 10. What the convenience cost, listed and not explained:
//
//     Your door now depends on things you can't see.
//     Your phone.
//     Software.
//     A connection.
//     And none of them stop at your house →
//
// Three nouns, no sentences around them. The story still does not say
// where the software runs, who operates it, or what any of it connects
// to — only that the door no longer works by itself.
//
// The picture moves in on the phone and the lock: x 65-655, y 307-972 of
// the 1181x1332 file, a transform on the element already on screen. The
// two of them and the gap between them are the subject now, but the lit
// glass, the door frame, the wood and the planter are all still in shot,
// so it stays plainly the same door. The dependencies arrive in the
// words; nothing is drawn on the picture — no signal, no cloud, no
// arrow, no label.
//
// SCENE 11. The first thing outside the house:
//
//     Your phone doesn't open the door by itself.
//     Somewhere else, software has to work.
//     A service has to respond.
//     A company has to keep it running.
//     So who are you trusting now? →
//
// Three plain facts and a question. Nothing about cybersecurity, third
// or fourth parties, supply chains, vendor management, or how any of it
// is built. The reader only has to realise that their front door now
// depends on something operated somewhere else.
//
// The picture is the fifth supplied asset: the same door and the same
// phone at the same lock, with one link out to a service and the company
// that runs it. One dependency, not a network of them — no second hop,
// no extra nodes, no labels, nothing drawn on it.
//
// It is landscape (1536x1024) where every other picture here is
// portrait, so this beat gives the copy column exactly the width the
// other beats give it — nothing in the text moves when it lands — and
// hands the picture the rest of the row instead of the narrow portrait
// track, which a 1.5 image would otherwise sit in at half the size.
//
// SCENE 12. Where the trust actually sits now:
//
//     Not just the lock anymore.
//     You're trusting the company behind it.
//     To keep the software working.
//     To keep the service available.
//     To let you back in.
//     And they don't work alone →
//
// One new realisation and no more: the trust moved from an object in the
// house to an organisation outside it. Still nothing about third or
// fourth parties, vendor risk, supply chains or cybersecurity.
//
// The picture shifts onto the company: same file, a transform on the
// element already on screen. The building is the largest thing in frame
// now, but the door, the phone at its lock and the whole run of light
// between them stay in shot — the beat is that the trust moved, not that
// the house did. Nothing is added to the picture: no second company, no
// chain, no label, no icon.
//
// SCENE 13. One more step, and the reader is the one who recognises it:
//
//     The company you trust depends on someone else.
//     Another company helps keep the service running.
//     You may never see them.
//     You may never know their name.
//     But your door depends on them too. →
//
// The same shape as the plumber bringing someone — deliberately not said
// out loud, because the reader made that connection on their own once
// already and will make it again faster. Still no vendor, third party,
// fourth party, supply chain, cybersecurity or risk.
//
// The picture does not move a pixel. That is the beat: the reader is
// told the company depends on somebody else, and the picture shows
// nobody else — the same window, the same door, the same run of light,
// the same one company. The absence is the point, so there is nothing to
// reveal and nothing to move toward.
//
// The turn on scene 13 is real and focusable and wired to nothing,
// because scene 14 does not exist yet — the same convention
// where-is-the-5g.astro and who-chose-what-you-saw.astro use for their
// unbuilt scenes.
//
// Still deliberately absent until the story is finished: a
// LearningResource, a share card, and a sitemap entry (see NOT_INDEXABLE
// in astro.config.mjs). The manifest's question, shortAnswer and
// keyTakeaway are still marked provisional there, and are still rendered
// nowhere.
const story = storyBySlug('security-didnt-keep-changing')!;
---

<BaseLayout title={storyTitle(story)} description={story.description}>
  <div class="door" data-door data-stage="1">
    <div class="door__stage shell">
      <div class="door__copy">
        <!-- Announced when it changes, because the change is the story
             moving. One group today; the same container the later beats
             will replace their lines inside. -->
        <div class="door__lines" aria-live="polite" tabindex="-1">
          <h1 class="door__opening">
            <span data-line="one">
              <span>Security didn't keep changing.</span>
              <span>What you trusted did.</span>
            </span>

            <span data-line="two" hidden>You leave home.</span>
            <span data-line="three" hidden>Because you trust the lock.</span>
            <span data-line="four" hidden>A pipe starts leaking.</span>

            <!-- The opening's couplet construction, reused deliberately:
                 the shape of the sentence is the callback. -->
            <span data-line="five" hidden>
              <span>The lock didn't change.</span>
              <span>Who you trusted did.</span>
            </span>

            <span data-line="six" hidden>He arrives.</span>
            <span data-line="seven" hidden>You didn't choose him.</span>
            <span data-line="eight" hidden>Trust can travel.</span>
            <span data-line="nine" hidden>You replace the lock.</span>
            <span data-line="ten" hidden>Your door now depends on things you can't see.</span>
            <span data-line="eleven" hidden>Your phone doesn't open the door by itself.</span>
            <span data-line="twelve" hidden>Not just the lock anymore.</span>
            <span data-line="thirteen" hidden>The company you trust depends on someone else.</span>
          </h1>

          <p class="door__sub" data-line="one">
            And you probably didn't notice when it happened.
          </p>

          <!-- Three steps in the order they happen, each on its own line,
               so the sequence reads as a sequence rather than a sentence. -->
          <p class="door__sub door__steps" data-line="two" hidden>
            <span>You close the door.</span>
            <span>You lock it.</span>
            <span>Then you walk away.</span>
          </p>

          <p class="door__sub door__steps" data-line="three" hidden>
            <span>You don't stay behind to guard the door.</span>
            <span>You expect the lock to do its job after you've walked away.</span>
          </p>

          <p class="door__sub door__steps" data-line="four" hidden>
            <span>You call a plumber.</span>
            <span>You won't be home when he arrives.</span>
            <span>So you leave him a key.</span>
          </p>

          <p class="door__sub door__steps" data-line="five" hidden>
            <span>The same key still opens the same door.</span>
            <span>But now someone else can use it.</span>
          </p>

          <p class="door__sub door__steps" data-line="six" hidden>
            <span>But he isn't alone.</span>
            <span>He brought someone to help.</span>
            <span>You've never met him.</span>
          </p>

          <p class="door__sub door__steps" data-line="seven" hidden>
            <span>You didn't check him.</span>
            <span>You don't even know him.</span>
            <span>But the plumber does.</span>
          </p>

          <!-- The beat's own conclusion, and the first thing in the story
               the reader is meant to carry rather than just watch. It sits
               between the quiet lines and the turn, in the story's serif
               at full ink but under the headline's size — the same type,
               one step down. Set as two lines because the sentence turns
               on its second half. -->
          <p class="door__lands" data-line="seven" hidden>
            <span>So now you're trusting someone</span>
            <span>because someone you trust trusts him.</span>
          </p>

          <p class="door__sub door__steps" data-line="eight" hidden>
            <span>You chose the plumber.</span>
            <span>He chose someone else.</span>
            <span>And his choice entered your home too.</span>
          </p>

          <p class="door__sub door__steps" data-line="nine" hidden>
            <span>No key this time.</span>
            <span>Your phone opens the door.</span>
            <span>It feels simpler.</span>
          </p>

          <p class="door__sub door__steps" data-line="ten" hidden>
            <span>Your phone.</span>
            <span>Software.</span>
            <span>A connection.</span>
          </p>

          <p class="door__sub door__steps" data-line="eleven" hidden>
            <span>Somewhere else, software has to work.</span>
            <span>A service has to respond.</span>
            <span>A company has to keep it running.</span>
          </p>

          <p class="door__sub door__steps" data-line="twelve" hidden>
            <span>You're trusting the company behind it.</span>
            <span>To keep the software working.</span>
            <span>To keep the service available.</span>
            <span>To let you back in.</span>
          </p>

          <p class="door__sub door__steps" data-line="thirteen" hidden>
            <span>Another company helps keep the service running.</span>
            <span>You may never see them.</span>
            <span>You may never know their name.</span>
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- The same turn treatment every story in the library uses:
             serif, the saffron circle, the quiet underline on hover. -->
        <button type="button" class="door__turn" data-line="one" data-onward>
          Start with your front door <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="door__turn" data-line="two" data-further hidden>
          Why? <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="door__turn" data-line="three" data-onward3 hidden>
          Then someone else needs to get in <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="door__turn" data-line="four" data-onward4 hidden>
          What changed? <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="door__turn" data-line="five" data-onward5 hidden>
          Then he arrives <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="door__turn" data-line="six" data-onward6 hidden>
          And yet he's about to enter your home. <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="door__turn" data-line="seven" data-onward7 hidden>
          And that's only one step away <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="door__turn" data-line="eight" data-onward8 hidden>
          Now put the key away <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="door__turn" data-line="nine" data-onward9 hidden>
          But something else just changed. <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="door__turn" data-line="ten" data-onward10 hidden>
          And none of them stop at your house <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="door__turn" data-line="eleven" data-onward11 hidden>
          So who are you trusting now? <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="door__turn" data-line="twelve" data-onward12 hidden>
          And they don't work alone <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="door__turn" data-line="thirteen" data-onward13 hidden>
          But your door depends on them too. <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </button>
      </div>

      <!-- Scene 1's composition, then the door both later beats share. -->
      <div class="door__visual">
        <div class="door__scene">
          <Image
            class="door__art"
            src={doorScene1}
            alt="A front door, with the lock, the people, the phone, the cloud, the building, the camera and a chain of smaller suppliers connected to it all around."
            widths={[560, 760, 1000, 1336, 1672]}
            sizes="(max-width: 979px) 92vw, 52vw"
            loading="eager"
            fetchpriority="high"
            decoding="async"
            data-line="one"
          />

          <!-- One window for the last seven beats. The door whole in scene
               2, moved in on in scene 3, the same porch with someone at
               it for scenes 4 and 5, two people on it in scene 6, and the
               two of them closer in scene 7, and back out again in scene
               8 — every move a transform on the element already there,
               never a swap. All three files
               are 1122x1402, so the place never shifts between beats.
               Served at full width because the close-up magnifies 3.3x
               and a column-sized file would go soft under that. -->
          <div class="door__frame" data-line="two three four five six seven eight" hidden>
            <Image
              class="door__art door__plain"
              src={doorScene2}
              alt="An ordinary front door, closed, with a lock and a handle."
              widths={[1122]}
              sizes="1122px"
              loading="eager"
              decoding="async"
              data-line="two three"
            />

            <!-- The same porch, same size, same file dimensions, with
                 someone at the door. It lands in the window the door
                 already occupied, so the beat is a person arriving at a
                 place the reader knows rather than a new picture. -->
            <Image
              class="door__art door__plain"
              src={doorScene4}
              alt="A plumber standing at the front door with a toolbag, reaching for the handle."
              widths={[1122]}
              sizes="1122px"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
              data-line="four five"
              hidden
            />

            <!-- The same porch again, same 1122x1402 frame, with a second
                 person on it. Shown whole and unpushed: scene 5 had
                 stepped in a little, and coming back out is what makes
                 room for somebody who was not expected. -->
            <Image
              class="door__art door__plain"
              src={doorScene6}
              alt="Two workers in the same uniform standing at the front door, one reaching for the handle, the other waiting behind him with a toolbag."
              widths={[1122]}
              sizes="1122px"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
              data-line="six seven eight"
              hidden
            />
          </div>

          <!-- The same door with a smart lock on it, and a phone at the
               lock — whole in scene 9, moved in on in scene 10. Its own
               window, because this file is 1181x1332 where the other
               three are 1122x1402: at that ratio it fills the box exactly
               at rest, so nothing is cropped or letterboxed until the
               beat that means to move. -->
          <div class="door__lockframe" data-line="nine ten" hidden>
            <Image
              class="door__art door__lockshot"
              src={doorScene9}
              alt="The same front door, now with a keypad lock above the handle, and a hand holding a phone up to it."
              widths={[1181]}
              sizes="1181px"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
          </div>

          <!-- The same door and the same phone at the same lock, and one
               thing beyond the house: a link out to a service, and the
               company that runs it. Whole in scene 11, shifted onto the
               company in scene 12. Landscape where the door assets are
               portrait, so it has its own window at its own ratio and
               takes the width of the row rather than the portrait
               column. -->
          <div class="door__cloudframe" data-line="eleven twelve thirteen" hidden>
            <Image
              class="door__art door__cloudshot"
              src={doorScene11}
              alt="The same front door with a phone at its lock, connected by a line of light to a cloud, and from the cloud to an office building."
              widths={[1536]}
              sizes="1536px"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .door {
    padding-bottom: clamp(60px, 9vh, 110px);
  }

  /* The library's opening grid, at the same max-width and the same top
     padding, so this story opens on the page at the height the others do.
     The gap is tighter than the two-beat stories use and the copy column
     is capped at its own measure, so the sentence and the door read as
     one composition rather than as two columns with a corridor between
     them. */
  .door__stage {
    max-width: 1180px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 5rem) 0 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    align-items: center;
    gap: clamp(24px, 4vh, 36px);
  }

  .door__copy,
  .door__visual {
    /* Grid items default to min-width: auto, which lets a long
       unbreakable line push a column wider than its track. */
    min-width: 0;
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* Focused when a beat lands and there is no turn left to hand focus to;
     it should never draw a ring of its own for that. */
  .door__lines:focus {
    outline: none;
  }

  /* The story's primary line, at the size and weight "Looks real." and
     "You chose this." carry in this slot. Deliberately not larger: the
     second half is the line that has to land, and it only lands if the
     whole statement, the line under it and the turn are visible at once. */
  .door__opening {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 17ch;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: clamp(27px, 3.2vw, 38px);
    line-height: 1.18;
    letter-spacing: -0.028em;
  }

  /* The couplet: the claim, then the correction under it. The second line
     takes the quieter ink the other stories give the half of a pair that
     turns the first half around. */
  .door__opening > span,
  .door__opening > span > span {
    display: block;
  }

  .door__opening > span > span:nth-child(2) {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .door__sub {
    margin: clamp(14px, 2vh, 20px) 0 0;
    max-width: 34ch;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* Where a beat reaches its own conclusion. The story's serif at full
     ink, one step down from the headline, so it reads as the sentence
     that matters on the screen without competing with the line that
     opened it. Two blocks, because the thought turns on its second half. */
  .door__lands {
    margin: clamp(16px, 2.4vh, 22px) 0 0;
    max-width: 30ch;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: clamp(19px, 1.7vw, 22px);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.34;
